WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.091
Good morning Ziad.

00:00:01.091 --> 00:00:03.032
It's Sunday, October eighteenth.

00:00:03.032 --> 00:00:05.033
Let's get you set up for the day.

00:00:05.033 --> 00:00:07.276
Bushwick gets a crisp, bright Sunday.

00:00:07.276 --> 00:00:13.218
It's sunny all day with zero chance of rain, topping out around fifty-nine degrees this afternoon.

00:00:13.218 --> 00:00:21.768
Tonight it drops hard, down to about forty-one, and with a light wind out of the north it'll feel closer to the mid-thirties by late evening.

00:00:21.768 --> 00:00:26.922
So wear a real jacket if you're heading out later, and maybe a hat for the walk home.

00:00:26.922 --> 00:00:32.440
The sun sets a little after six, which means the good light runs from about four-thirty on.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.168
Your calendar is wide open until the evening.

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:44.142
From five to nine you've got a get-together with friends, and right now the food, the activity, and the movie are all still listed as to be decided.

00:00:44.142 --> 00:00:51.903
If you're the one organizing, it might be worth locking in at least the food before lunch so nobody's stuck debating it at five.

00:00:51.903 --> 00:00:53.844
Everything before then is yours.

00:00:53.844 --> 00:00:56.148
Here are three stories worth a minute.

00:00:56.148 --> 00:00:56.997
First, France.

00:00:56.997 --> 00:01:03.242
Protests over the rising cost of living, joined by a lot of students, turned violent in several cities.

00:01:03.242 --> 00:01:06.638
In Limoges, a spire on the historic town hall collapsed.

00:01:06.638 --> 00:01:15.733
A town hall in Marseille was stormed, a tourism office in Lyon was set on fire, and dozens of people were arrested in Paris after clashes with police.

00:01:15.733 --> 00:01:21.251
It's worth watching, because cost of living anger has been building across Europe all year.

00:01:21.251 --> 00:01:25.738
Second, Pope Leo the Fourteenth is out of the hospital after lung surgery.

00:01:25.738 --> 00:01:29.497
He's recovering and has already gone back to celebrating Mass.

00:01:29.497 --> 00:01:31.559
Third, a reality check on the A.I.

00:01:31.559 --> 00:01:32.407
money machine.

00:01:32.407 --> 00:01:39.259
A note from the investment bank Jefferies, making the rounds this weekend, projects that earnings growth for A.I.

00:01:39.259 --> 00:01:48.779
related stocks worldwide will fall from nearly one hundred percent this year to about thirty percent next year, and around twenty-two percent the year after.

00:01:48.779 --> 00:01:55.145
That's still strong growth, but it's the kind of slowdown that can rattle a market priced for perfection.

00:01:55.145 --> 00:01:57.146
Meanwhile the models keep coming.

00:01:57.146 --> 00:02:04.968
DeepSeek just released a new open model built for agents, with live web search baked in and a context window of a million tokens.

00:02:04.968 --> 00:02:07.818
There are two good options close to home today.

00:02:07.818 --> 00:02:19.521
If you want to stay in the neighborhood, there's a guided Bushwick street art walk starting at two o'clock on Meserole Street, covering more than twenty-five murals with a local artist leading.

00:02:19.521 --> 00:02:25.705
It's about thirty-five to forty dollars, and there's an optional Venezuelan brunch beforehand at noon.

00:02:25.705 --> 00:02:29.101
Tickets were running low, so check before you walk over.

00:02:29.101 --> 00:02:38.378
If you'd rather browse for free, the FAD Market is wrapping up its weekend run at Brooklyn Brewery in Williamsburg, with local makers selling their work.

00:02:38.378 --> 00:02:40.803
Admission is free, and dogs are welcome.

00:02:40.803 --> 00:02:43.289
Today's challenge is a twelve frame walk.

00:02:43.289 --> 00:02:49.110
Pick one block, any block, and before your five o'clock plans, take exactly twelve photos on it.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:53.234
The rule is that no two frames can share the same angle or distance.

00:02:53.234 --> 00:02:59.903
One from the ground, one through a window, one tight on a texture, one wide from across the street, and so on.

00:02:59.903 --> 00:03:07.058
Twelve frames forces you to stop shooting the obvious picture and start hunting for the ones you'd normally walk past.

00:03:07.058 --> 00:03:11.848
With this clear, low autumn sun, the shadows will do a lot of the work for you.

00:03:11.848 --> 00:03:14.880
Here's how a railroad schedule gave us time zones.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:19.488
Before the eighteen-eighties, every American town set its clocks by the sun.

00:03:19.488 --> 00:03:30.221
Noon was whenever the sun was highest over that particular town, so when it was noon in New York, it was a few minutes earlier in Philadelphia and a few minutes later in Boston.

00:03:30.221 --> 00:03:32.282
For people on foot, that was fine.

00:03:32.282 --> 00:03:33.980
For railroads, it was chaos.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:41.074
Companies ran on dozens of different local times, and timetables were a nightmare to read and dangerous to get wrong.

00:03:41.074 --> 00:03:50.230
So on November eighteenth, eighteen eighty-three, the railroads in the United States and Canada simply agreed to switch to a handful of standard zones.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:56.354
Stations reset their clocks at noon, and in many towns the hands jumped or stopped for a few minutes.

00:03:56.354 --> 00:03:59.022
People later called it the day of two noons.

00:03:59.022 --> 00:04:00.659
Here's the surprising part.

00:04:00.659 --> 00:04:05.268
The government didn't make those zones official law until nineteen eighteen.

00:04:05.268 --> 00:04:10.907
For thirty-five years, the country basically ran on railroad time because the trains said so.

00:04:10.907 --> 00:04:12.119
That's your morning.

00:04:12.119 --> 00:04:16.000
Go make some pictures, and have a great night with your friends.
